<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Turkic Khaganate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as First Khaganate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Turkic Khaganate was the empire founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashina-clan">Ashina clan</a> in 552 after the overthrow of the Rouran. It quickly extended across the Eurasian steppe from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, before splitting into eastern and western khaganates. It is the first phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The khaganate was founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashina-clan">Ashina clan</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumin-qaghan">Bumin Qaghan</a> and his brother Istami. In 546 Bumin made a preemptive strike against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> groups planning to revolt against their overlords, the Rouran, and expected a Rouran princess in marriage as his reward. The Rouran khagan Yujiulu Anagui rebuked him, calling him a blacksmith slave.</p><p>Chinese chronicles record the phrase, and some take it to show that the Turks specialised in metallurgy, though it may describe a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> rather than literal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumin-qaghan">Bumin</a> allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> by marrying Princess Changle and in 552 defeated Anagui north of Huaihuang, effectively ending the Rouran, who had dominated the eastern steppe for a century and a half.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumin-qaghan">Bumin</a> declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illig-qaghan">Illig Khagan</a> at Otuken but died within months, and the empire was divided between his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqan-qaghan">Muqan Qaghan</a>, who ruled the east and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>, and Istami, who bore the title Yabghu of the West. In 557 Istami allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> to destroy the Hephthalites.</p><p>In 576 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch-strait">Kerch Strait</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, and five years later laid siege to Chersonesus. They sent embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, one of which arrived in 568 and discussed an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a>, but a Byzantine embassy of 575-576 was received with hostility, and the alliance broke down. In 589 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> attacked and defeated the Turks.</p><p>When the fourth ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taspar-qaghan">Taspar Qaghan</a>, died in 581, the realm split over the succession, and four rivals claimed the title, played off against each other by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang. Istami's son Tardu made a bid for independence, and Ishbara in the east acknowledged the suzerainty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang of Sui</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> diplomacy incited a revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> vassals that ended Tardu's reign in 603, leaving an eastern and a western khaganate. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illig-qaghan">Illig Qaghan</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 626 and agreed to withdraw, but in 630 a Tang army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jing">Li Jing</a> defeated the Eastern Turks at the Battle of Yinshan. The Western khaganate, known as the Onoq, fell to the Tang general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-dingfang">Su Dingfang</a> in 657.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Tang rule left a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashina-clan">Ashina clan</a> as a puppet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, and in 657 the Tang installed two khagans over the ten arrows, the tribes of the Western Turks, with Chinese titles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilterish-qaghan">Ilterish Qaghan</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-turkic-khaganate">Second Turkic Khaganate</a> after rebelling against the Tang, and it lasted until the Uyghur Khaganate overthrew it in 744. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> script was invented in the first half of the sixth century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a> spoke a Siberian Turkic language that was the direct predecessor of the Orkhon Turkic of the Second Khaganate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Turkic Khaganate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>First Khaganate</b> at its peak was likely the largest contiguous territorial entity on earth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 552 the Göktürks took Mongolia and formed the <b>First Turkic Khaganate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The titles khagan, for the supreme ruler, and khatun, for his consort, became established at the Rouran court and were taken over by the <b>Turkic Khaganate</b>, which arose among the Rouran's own subjects, and later by the Uyghurs, the Khazars and the Mongols.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rouran conquered the northern oasis states in the late 5th century, the Hephthalites held the Tarim until about 560, and then the <b>First Turkic Khaganate</b> took Turfan and Kucha; Kucha’s royal family served the Western Turks with tribute and troops.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In 552 Bumin Qaghan overthrew the Rouran and founded the <b>First Turkic Khaganate</b>, which within a generation stretched from Manchuria to the Black Sea.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>First Turkic Khaganate</b> split around 603, the eastern khagans of the Ashina clan held the Mongolian steppe, with their sacred centre at Otuken in the Khangai mountains.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a></li></ul></section>
